WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.536
Cryptography is an engineering control, not a magic layer placed on top of an application at the end.

2
00:00:06.536 --> 00:00:11.257
It supports specific security goals such as confidentiality, integrity, authenticity, and trust decisions.

3
00:00:11.257 --> 00:00:15.978
The first question is always what boundary the design is trying to protect.

4
00:00:15.978 --> 00:00:18.520
Stored data may need confidentiality at rest.

5
00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:20.698
A software update may need authenticity.

6
00:00:20.698 --> 00:00:24.330
A message between services may need integrity and origin assurance.

7
00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:28.324
A connection may need identity verification before sensitive data is exchanged.

8
00:00:28.324 --> 00:00:31.592
Strong algorithms are only one part of the system.

9
00:00:31.592 --> 00:00:39.218
The program still needs correct keys, correct library use, validated inputs and outputs, clear error handling, and safe behavior when verification

10
00:00:39.218 --> 00:00:39.581
fails.

11
00:00:39.581 --> 00:00:46.117
In C++ systems, cryptography often appears around data storage, communication, update validation, message authentication, license or identity checks,

12
00:00:46.117 --> 00:00:48.296
and integration with platform trust stores.

13
00:00:48.296 --> 00:00:51.927
Each use should be tied to a concrete security decision.

14
00:00:51.927 --> 00:00:54.832
The hard part is usually not the mathematics.

15
00:00:54.832 --> 00:01:03.184
It is choosing an approved library, using the right primitive for the goal, protecting keys, managing randomness and nonces, and making failure paths

16
00:01:03.184 --> 00:01:03.547
predictable.

17
00:01:03.547 --> 00:01:06.452
A healthy crypto boundary is small and reviewable.

18
00:01:06.452 --> 00:01:15.167
Reviewers should be able to answer what is protected, what is trusted, what key material is involved, and what the application does when validation

19
00:01:15.167 --> 00:01:15.531
fails.

20
00:01:15.531 --> 00:01:20.977
That engineering discipline keeps cryptography connected to the real system it is supposed to protect.

21
00:01:20.977 --> 00:01:28.240
It also prevents teams from treating crypto as decoration instead of a control with inputs, outputs, owners, and operational responsibilities.
